const e={note:"Hebrew is where letter-mysticism is most fully worked out, and this hall’s currents try to show the machinery rather than gesture at it. Three cautions. Datings here are unusually wide: the Sefer Yetzirah has been placed anywhere between the second and the eighth century and survives in three recensions that differ substantially, and the currents give the range rather than a number. Second, the tradition’s own attributions and the philological ones frequently disagree — the Zohar is ascribed to Shimon bar Yochai in the second century and to Moses de León in the thirteenth — and both are stated with the parties named. Third, the Christian Cabala and the nineteenth-century Western reception are treated here as reception history: they changed what the material became in Europe, and they are not Jewish tradition, and the currents say which is which.",themes:[{slug:"sefer-yetzirah",title:"The Book of Formation",native:"ספר יצירה",translit:"Sefer Yetzirah",summary:"Under two thousand words that hand the tradition its two permanent instruments — the decade and the alphabet — and survive in three recensions that do not agree with one another.",paragraphs:["Sefer Yetzirah opens by announcing thirty-two wondrous paths of wisdom, and then names them: ten sefirot belimah and twenty-two foundation letters, divided into three mothers, seven doubles and twelve simples. It is the first Hebrew text to use sefirah as a technical term, and the sefirot it describes are not yet the divine emanations of later kabbalah. They are closer to dimensions — the depth of beginning and the depth of end, the depth of good and the depth of evil, height, depth, east, west, north, south — whose ends are joined, in the book's own image, as a flame is bound to a coal. The word belimah is lifted from Job 26:7, where God hangs the earth on belimah, nothing; the book reads it as beli mah, without-what. Its instruction at 1:8 is the sentence the whole tradition afterwards quotes: restrain your mouth from speaking and your heart from thinking, and if your heart runs, return to the place.","The three mothers are aleph, mem and shin, and every recension gives them to the same elements: aleph to air, mem to water, shin to fire. The rationale is phonetic before it is cosmological — mem hums with the mouth closed, shin hisses, and aleph is the breath that holds the balance between them, a decree between two scales. There is no fourth element among the mothers. Earth, where the book speaks of it, is formed out of water rather than standing beside it, which is why later systems that want four elements have to add one. From the mothers the book derives its three-fold divisions elsewhere: heaven, earth and the air between; heat, cold and the temperate season; head, belly and the chest that mediates. The pattern of two contraries reconciled by a third runs through the whole treatise and is arguably its single organising idea.","The seven doubles are bet, gimel, dalet, kaf, pe, resh and tav — the six letters sounded hard or soft, with resh added — and to them the book assigns seven planets, seven days, the seven gates in the head (two eyes, two ears, two nostrils and the mouth), and seven pairs of opposites: life and death, peace and evil, wisdom and folly, wealth and poverty, grace and ugliness, seed and desolation, dominion and servitude. The twelve simples carry the twelve months, the twelve signs, the twelve diagonal boundaries of the cube, and twelve faculties — sight, hearing, smell, speech, taste, coition, action, movement, anger, laughter, thought and sleep. The zodiacal series is stable across recensions, running in alphabetical order from he for Aries at Nisan. The planetary series is not, and readers should be plain about this: the Gra–Ari text runs the doubles in descending Chaldean order, the long and Saadian recensions differ again, and the ordering familiar from Western esoteric writing belongs to the Hermetic Order of the Golden Dawn rather than to any Hebrew manuscript.","Then the machinery. The letters are set in a wheel with two hundred and thirty-one gates — the number of unordered pairs drawn from twenty-two — and turned forward and backward, so that every letter is combined with every other. The book counts the combinations by a series that runs two stones building two houses, three stones six houses, four stones twenty-four, five a hundred and twenty, six seven hundred and twenty, seven five thousand and forty, and stops with the remark that from here on the mouth cannot speak nor the ear hear. That is a statement of the factorial function, and it is among the earliest in any language. The closing paragraph turns to Abraham, who looked, saw, understood, probed, engraved and hewed, and with whom a covenant was made in the tongue and in the flesh. What was being engraved and hewed became a question in its own right: Sanhedrin 65b reports that Rava created a man and sent him to Rabbi Zeira, and that Rav Hanina and Rav Oshaia occupied themselves each Sabbath eve with hilkhot yetzirah and made a calf, and later readers took the Talmud to be describing this book at work.","The text itself is the difficulty. It survives in three recensions — a short one and a long one, each of six chapters, and an eight-chapter arrangement transmitted with Saadia Gaon's commentary — and they differ not only in wording but in the order of sentences and the content of the attributions. Ithamar Gruenwald published a preliminary critical edition in 1971; A. Peter Hayman's edition of 2004 prints the recensions in parallel and argues that the divergence may run back past any single original, so that asking for the date of Sefer Yetzirah may be asking a badly formed question. The datings offered span seven centuries. The tradition ascribes the book to Abraham on the strength of its closing paragraph, with a second line giving it to Akiva; Scholem and most of the field place it in the talmudic centuries, between the third and the sixth; Steven Wasserstrom and Y. Tzvi Langermann have argued for a ninth-century Islamic milieu. The first firm evidence of any kind is the cluster of tenth-century commentaries — Saadia in Arabic, Dunash ibn Tamim at Kairouan, Shabbetai Donnolo in southern Italy — and everything earlier is inference."],terms:[{native:"יצירה",translit:"yetsirah",gloss:"formation, fashioning; the book's title word, and the term the Talmud uses at Sanhedrin 65b for whatever it is that makes a creature"},{native:"בלימה",translit:"belimah",gloss:"the qualifier attached to the ten sefirot; taken from Job 26:7, where the earth hangs on nothing, and read by the book as beli mah, 'without what'"},{native:"אמות",translit:"immot",gloss:"mothers; the three letters aleph, mem and shin, given to air, water and fire in every recension alike"},{native:"כפולות",translit:"kefulot",gloss:"doubles; the seven letters sounded hard or soft — bet, gimel, dalet, kaf, pe, resh, tav — and the point at which the recensions diverge most sharply"},{native:"פשוטות",translit:"peshutot",gloss:"simples; the remaining twelve letters, run in order through the months and the signs from he at Aries"},{native:"גלגל",translit:"galgal",gloss:"wheel, sphere; the figure in which the letters are set and turned to yield the 231 gates — every pair that can be drawn from twenty-two"}],texts:[{title:"Sefer Yetzirah, ed. A. Peter Hayman (Mohr Siebeck, 2004)",note:"The short, long and Saadian recensions printed in parallel with translation and text-critical commentary; the edition in which the recension problem can actually be inspected rather than asserted."},{title:"Ithamar Gruenwald, 'A Preliminary Critical Edition of Sefer Yezira', Israel Oriental Studies 1 (1971)",note:"The earlier attempt at a critical text, still cited, and the point from which modern work on the recensions starts."},{title:"Saadia Gaon, commentary on Sefer Yetzirah (Arabic, c. 931)",note:"The earliest datable commentary and the witness to the eight-chapter arrangement; philosophical rather than mystical in its reading, and evidence for the text's existence by the tenth century."},{title:"Shabbetai Donnolo, Sefer Ḥakhmoni (c. 982)",note:"A commentary from southern Italy, roughly contemporary with Saadia and Dunash ibn Tamim of Kairouan; the three together are the first firm ground under the book."},{title:"Eleazar of Worms, commentary on Sefer Yetzirah",note:"Early thirteenth century, from the circle of the Hasidei Ashkenaz; reads the book as a manual of letter-combination, and carries the instructions on the making of a golem discussed in the Practical Side."}]},{slug:"gematria",title:"The Reckoning of Letters",native:"גימטריה",translit:"gimaṭriyya",summary:"Every Hebrew word is also a number, an acronym and a cipher waiting to be turned — and the tradition's better practitioners knew exactly how cheap a coincidence is.",paragraphs:["Hebrew has no separate numerals. Aleph to tet carry the units, yod to tsadi the tens, qof to tav the hundreds, so every word written is also a sum, and every sum can be compared with another. The system is attested in use on Hasmonean coinage of the second and first centuries BCE, and is generally held to have been taken over from Greek practice, where alphabetic numerals are older. The name of the technique is itself Greek — gematria, from geōmetria, though grammateia has also been proposed and the etymology is not settled; Saul Lieberman's Hellenism in Jewish Palestine placed the rabbinic use of it firmly among Greek rhetorical and exegetical habits. Nor is the idea Jewish in origin. The display inscription of Sargon II at Khorsabad states that the king built the city wall 16,283 cubits, the number of his name, and that is an Assyrian text of the eighth century BCE.","The classical rabbinic examples are worth having exactly, because they are constantly cited loosely. Nedarim 32a reads the three hundred and eighteen trained men Abram leads out at Genesis 14:14 as one man, Eliezer, whose name sums to 318. Rashi, on Genesis 32:5, takes Jacob's message that he has sojourned with Laban — garti — and reads the word's value of 613 as a claim to have kept the six hundred and thirteen commandments. The aphorism at Eruvin 65a that when wine goes in a secret comes out rests on yayin and sod both coming to seventy. The Baraita of the Thirty-Two Rules, ascribed by tradition to Rabbi Eliezer ben Yose ha-Gelili and dated by most scholars considerably later, lists gematria and notarikon among the hermeneutic rules. In nearly every classical case the number is asmakhta, a support hung on a reading already arrived at by other means. It ornaments an argument; it does not carry one.","Notarikon works on letters as initials rather than as values, and runs in both directions. Shabbat 105a reads the anokhi that opens the Decalogue as a sentence in Aramaic — ana nafshi ketavit yehavit, I myself wrote and gave — and then, having done so, offers a second reading in which the same letters are taken in reverse order. The technique also generates: pardes, the orchard the four sages entered at Hagigah 14b, was made in the Middle Ages to stand for peshat, remez, derash and sod, the four levels of reading, a construction that is medieval however ancient the word. The tradition names its own authorities the same way, which is why Rashi and Rambam are the ordinary names of Solomon ben Isaac and Moses ben Maimon.","Temurah exchanges one letter for another by a fixed rule. Its best-known form, atbash, pairs the first letter with the last, the second with the second-last, and so on — and it is the one technique whose use is visible inside the biblical text itself. Jeremiah 25:26 and 51:41 write Sheshakh where Babel is meant, and Jeremiah 51:1 writes Lev Kamai where Kasdim is meant; both are atbash, and Rashi reads them so. Other schemes multiply: albam pairing the halves of the alphabet, atbach working by numerical complements, and the full tabulation of twenty-two ciphers set out in Moses Cordovero's Pardes Rimonim, completed at Safed in 1548. Sefer Yetzirah's wheel of 231 gates is the same instinct in its purest form — not a cipher for concealing a message but an exhaustive machine for turning letters against one another.","The later apparatus is elaborate and much of it is late. Mispar katan reduces each value to a single digit; mispar gadol assigns the five final forms the values five hundred to nine hundred, a scribal convention of the medieval and early modern period and no part of Talmudic or Masoretic usage; millui spells out each letter's name and sums that instead, which is what produces the four spellings of the Tetragrammaton valued 72, 63, 45 and 52 on which Lurianic doctrine turns. And here the honest caution belongs. With twenty-two letters, several methods and a free choice of which words to compare, agreements are abundant and prove nothing on their own; the strongest kabbalists used gematria to display a structure they held on other grounds. What is sometimes offered as the modern continuation of the practice is not one: the equidistant-letter-sequence claims published by Doron Witztum, Eliyahu Rips and Yoav Rosenberg in Statistical Science in 1994 were answered in the same journal in 1999 by Brendan McKay, Dror Bar-Natan, Maya Bar-Hillel and Gil Kalai, who reproduced the effect in Tolstoy, and that exchange is a dispute about statistics rather than an episode in the history of gematria."],terms:[{native:"גימטריה",translit:"gimaṭriyya",gloss:"interpretation by numerical value; the name is Greek, most often derived from geōmetria, and the derivation is not agreed"},{native:"נוטריקון",translit:"noṭariqon",gloss:"reading a word as a set of initials, or building a word from them; from Latin notarius, the shorthand-writer"},{native:"תמורה",translit:"temurah",gloss:"exchange; the substitution of letters by a fixed rule, of which atbash is one scheme among many"},{native:'אתב"ש',translit:"atbash",gloss:"the cipher pairing aleph with tav, bet with shin and so on inward; used inside the book of Jeremiah, where Babel is written Sheshakh"},{native:"מספר קטן",translit:"mispar qaṭan",gloss:"the 'small number': each letter reduced to a single digit, so that tav (400) counts as four"},{native:"מילוי",translit:"millui",gloss:"filling; spelling out a letter's name and summing that — the operation behind the four valuations of the Tetragrammaton at 72, 63, 45 and 52"}],texts:[{title:"The Baraita of the Thirty-Two Rules",note:"Ascribed by tradition to Rabbi Eliezer ben Yose ha-Gelili and dated by most scholars later, probably geonic; lists gematria and notarikon among the rules by which aggadah is expounded."},{title:"BT Nedarim 32a; BT Shabbat 105a; BT Eruvin 65a",note:"The three standard classical demonstrations: Eliezer as the 318 of Genesis 14:14, anokhi read as an Aramaic sentence, and the equality of wine and secret at seventy."},{title:"Jeremiah 25:26 and 51:41, with Rashi",note:"Sheshakh for Babel — atbash operating inside the biblical text, and, with Lev Kamai for Kasdim at Jeremiah 51:1, one of only two places where the cipher is not applied to Scripture from outside."},{title:"Moses Cordovero, Pardes Rimonim (completed Safed, 1548)",note:"Gates on letter-exchange and combination that tabulate the cipher-alphabets systematically; the point at which the techniques become a documented apparatus rather than scattered usages."},{title:"Statistical Science 9 (1994), 429–438, and 14 (1999), 150–173",note:"Witztum, Rips and Rosenberg's equidistant-letter-sequence paper and the refutation by McKay, Bar-Natan, Bar-Hillel and Kalai. Included here to mark what is not gematria: a twentieth-century statistical claim, answered on statistical grounds."}]},{slug:"merkavah",title:"The Chariot and the Palaces",native:"מעשה מרכבה",translit:"Ma'aseh Merkavah",summary:"Seven celestial halls, gatekeepers who must be shown a seal, and a warning about what to say when the marble looks like water.",paragraphs:["The subject begins with a single chapter. Ezekiel, by the river Chebar in the fifth year of the exile of Jehoiachin, sees a storm out of the north, four living creatures with four faces, wheels within wheels rimmed with eyes, a firmament, a throne of sapphire, and upon it a likeness as the appearance of a man, gleaming with hashmal — a word whose meaning was already obscure to the rabbis and remains disputed. The Mishnah at Hagigah 2:1 fences it: the Work of Creation is not expounded before two, and the Chariot not before one, and not even before one unless he is a sage who understands of his own knowledge. That is a restriction on teaching, not a denial that anything is there. Hagigah 14b then tells of the four who entered the orchard — Ben Azzai, who looked and died; Ben Zoma, who looked and was stricken; Elisha ben Abuyah, who cut the shoots; and Akiva, who entered in peace and departed in peace.","The Hekhalot literature takes the vision and makes it a route. Hekhalot Rabbati, Hekhalot Zutarti, Ma'aseh Merkavah, the Shi'ur Qomah and the book called 3 Enoch describe an ascent through seven palaces, each with its gate, each gate with its guardians, who must be shown the seal proper to that station before the traveller is admitted. Hymns carry him upward; the wrong word at the wrong threshold destroys him. The most memorable of the warnings turns on a visual trick: at the entrance to the sixth palace the pure marble stones give the appearance of water, and the traveller who cries out 'water, water' is destroyed for speaking falsely before the throne — a caution that appears both in Hagigah 14b, on Akiva's lips, and in the Hekhalot texts themselves. And there is an oddity nobody has explained away: the practitioners are called yordei merkavah, those who go down to the Chariot, though everything they describe is an ascent.","The Shi'ur Qomah is the hardest part of the corpus for later readers, and the most instructive. It gives the measurements of the divine body, limb by limb, in parasangs of a length so vast that the numbers cease to function as quantities at all; the tradition derives the figure from Psalm 147:5 — ve-rav koaḥ, “and abundant in power” — whose letters sum to 236 with the conjunctive vav counted, and to 230 without it. The effect is not anthropomorphism so much as its collapse, magnitude used to defeat the imagination it seems to serve. It was found intolerable in any case. Saadia handled it with visible discomfort; Maimonides, in a responsum, declared it no work of the Sages and fit to be destroyed. That a text of this kind circulated at all under rabbinic auspices is one of the reasons the Hekhalot corpus was so difficult for nineteenth- and twentieth-century scholarship to place.","Alongside the ascent runs its inversion. The Sar ha-Torah and Sar ha-Panim materials do not send the practitioner up; they bring an angel down, adjured by names to confer perfect and permanent memory of what has been learned. Peter Schäfer has read this strand as the most socially revealing in the corpus — Torah obtained by adjuration rather than by years in the academy, which is a promise made to people outside the academy. Presiding over the whole is Metatron, whose portrait in 3 Enoch is the boldest in the literature: Enoch taken up and transformed, enthroned, given seventy names and called the lesser YHWH. The danger in that title is registered inside the tradition at Hagigah 15a, where Elisha ben Abuyah sees Metatron seated and concludes that there are two powers in heaven, and Metatron is scourged with lashes of fire to establish that he is not one of them.","The scholarly ground here shifted decisively in 1981, when Schäfer published the Synopse zur Hekhalot-Literatur, printing seven manuscripts in parallel columns. What it showed is that these are not books. The material circulates as macroforms and microforms, recombining from copy to copy, with no stable text to edit, and every complete manuscript medieval and European — though the Genizah fragments Schäfer published in 1984 carry the literature back considerably further. The dating divides accordingly. Scholem, in Jewish Gnosticism, Merkabah Mysticism, and Talmudic Tradition (1960), took the corpus for the deposit of tannaitic circles in Roman Palestine from the third century onward and read the Hekhalot texts as evidence of a practised discipline. Schäfer and David Halperin argued for Babylonian and geonic redaction reaching into the ninth century, and Halperin's The Faces of the Chariot (1988) located the impulse in synagogue exposition of Ezekiel 1 rather than in an esoteric conventicle. A further question sits behind both: the Songs of the Sabbath Sacrifice from Qumran and Masada, edited by Carol Newsom in 1985, describe angelic liturgy before a throne-chariot in the first century BCE, and whether they are an ancestor of this literature or an independent growth from the same chapter of Ezekiel is not settled."],terms:[{native:"מרכבה",translit:"merkavah",gloss:"chariot; not a word Ezekiel uses of his vision — it is supplied by the tradition, and by 1 Chronicles 28:18 of the cherubim in the Temple"},{native:"היכלות",translit:"heikhalot",gloss:"palaces, halls; the seven through which the traveller passes, and the name given to the whole late-antique corpus"},{native:"יורדי מרכבה",translit:"yordei merkavah",gloss:"those who go down to the Chariot; the practitioners' own designation, and unexplained, since the journey described is upward"},{native:"חשמל",translit:"ḥashmal",gloss:"the gleaming substance of Ezekiel 1:4 and 1:27, obscure already to the rabbis, hedged about with warnings at Hagigah 13a, and revived in modern Hebrew as the word for electricity"},{native:"שיעור קומה",translit:"shi'ur qomah",gloss:"the measure of the stature; the reckoning of the divine body in parasangs, which Maimonides rejected as no work of the Sages"},{native:"שר התורה",translit:"sar ha-torah",gloss:"the Prince of the Torah; the angel adjured to descend and confer unforgetting knowledge of what has been studied — the corpus's inversion of the ascent"}],texts:[{title:"Ezekiel 1 and 10",note:"The vision by the river Chebar, dated in the text to the fifth year of Jehoiachin's exile; the chapter from which the entire tradition grows, and the one the Mishnah restricts."},{title:"Mishnah Hagigah 2:1; BT Hagigah 13a–15a",note:"The restriction on teaching the Chariot; the four who entered the orchard; the warning about the marble that looks like water; and Elisha ben Abuyah's two powers."},{title:"Hekhalot Rabbati",note:"The fullest narrative of the ascent through the seven palaces, with the gatekeepers, seals and hymns; speaks in the voices of Ishmael and Akiva, which no modern scholar takes as authorship."},{title:"3 Enoch (Sefer Hekhalot)",note:"Enoch's transformation into Metatron, the seventy names, and the title 'lesser YHWH'; usually placed in the fifth to sixth centuries, with the material older in parts."},{title:"Peter Schäfer, Synopse zur Hekhalot-Literatur (1981)",note:"Seven manuscripts printed in parallel. The edition that established there are no stable books in this corpus, only recombining macroforms — and that every witness is medieval and European."}]},{slug:"sefirot",title:"The Ten and the Book of Splendour",native:"עשר ספירות",translit:"Eser Sefirot",summary:"Ten emanations, a tree drawn to hold them, and a book in archaising Aramaic whose authorship has been argued for seven hundred years.",paragraphs:["Sefer Yetzirah has ten sefirot belimah, but they are not gods, powers or persons; the transformation into a structure of divine life happens elsewhere and later. Sefer ha-Bahir, which surfaces in Provence around the 1180s and speaks in the voice of Nehunya ben ha-Kanah of the first century, is where the ten first appear as powers within the Godhead, arrayed in an image the book calls a tree, with the tenth conceived as feminine and as the vessel of the rest. It is a strange text — abrupt, fragmentary, given to parables of kings and daughters and pearls — and it is also the first Jewish book to teach the transmigration of souls. Scholem edited and translated it into German in 1923; Daniel Abrams produced a critical edition in 1994; the oldest manuscript is Munich Cod. hebr. 209, copied in 1298, which is a century after the book was already being quoted.","The system settled into the form later readers know across the thirteenth century — Isaac the Blind in Provence, his students Ezra and Azriel at Gerona, and Nahmanides, whose commentary on the Torah embeds kabbalistic readings under the phrase 'by way of truth' and so carried them into the most respectable exegetical company available. Ten names in three groups: Keter, Chokhmah and Binah above; Chesed, Gevurah and Tiferet; Netzach, Hod and Yesod; and Malkhut, the Kingdom, at the foot, identified with the Shekhinah. Three columns — mercy on the right, judgment on the left, balance in the middle — and Da'at counted as a station on the middle pillar by those who reckon Keter outside the ten. Ein Sof, the Infinite beyond all attribute, is a term of these thirteenth-century circles rather than a biblical or rabbinic name, and its coinage is the moment at which the tradition acquires a Godhead about which nothing can be said standing behind a Godhead about which a great deal can be.","This makes the commandments consequential in a new way. If the sefirot are the divine life, and if the world below is arranged in correspondence with them, then an act performed below acts above: prayer with the right intention, a commandment done in the right frame, unites Tiferet with Malkhut and restores a flow that sin interrupts. That is the theurgic claim, and it is what separates this material from mere cosmological speculation. It also produces the tradition's most audacious language, in which the union of the divine masculine and feminine is described in the vocabulary of marriage, and the Sabbath eve is when it takes place.","The Zohar arrives in Castile in the 1280s as quires of a difficult, archaising Aramaic: a mystical midrash on the Torah in which Shimon bar Yochai and his companions walk the roads of Galilee and expound. It is not one work. The main body is accompanied by the Midrash ha-Ne'elam, the Sitrei Torah, the two Assemblies — Idra Rabba at Zohar III 127b and following, Idra Zuta at the master's death — and, from a later hand around the turn of the fourteenth century, the Ra'aya Meheimna and Tikkunei ha-Zohar. Its themes are the sefirot as a divine countenance described feature by feature, the Shekhinah in exile with Israel, the sitra ahra or Other Side as a structured realm of impurity rather than a mere absence, and the mirroring of the union above in the marriage bed below.","Who wrote it is a real argument, and it should be given as one. The tradition holds the book to be the teaching of Shimon bar Yochai in the second century, set down by his circle and hidden until its recovery in Spain. Scholem argued in 1941 that the main body is the work of one man, Moses de León of Guadalajara, who died in 1305: the Aramaic is constructed rather than inherited, with medieval Hebrew and Castilian idiom showing through, and Isaac of Acre's report — preserved in Abraham Zacuto's Sefer ha-Yuhasin — has de León's widow deny that any ancient manuscript ever existed. Later scholarship has moved on from single authorship: Yehuda Liebes, in 'How the Zohar Was Written' (1993), argues for a circle; Ronit Meroz for strata laid down over decades; Daniel Abrams contends that there is no Book of the Zohar at all before the printers made one, only a manuscript tradition. Nor is this simply scholars against believers — Jacob Emden's Mitpachat Sefarim, published at Altona in 1768, sifted the book from inside the tradition and judged much of it a late accretion on a genuinely ancient core. A second-century date for the whole is defended today by no academic historian, and held by the tradition still. The book was printed at Mantua and Cremona in 1558–60 over rabbinic objection, became the third canonical text of Judaism after the Bible and the Talmud, and is read aloud in Zohar circles in Sephardi, Mizrahi and Hasidic communities today, where recitation rather than analysis is the point."],terms:[{native:"ספירה",translit:"sefirah",gloss:"one of the ten; the word appears first in Sefer Yetzirah, where it does not yet mean a divine emanation, and its etymology — from counting, from sapphire, from telling — is argued"},{native:"אין סוף",translit:"Ein Sof",gloss:"the Infinite; a coinage of the Provençal and Geronese kabbalists of the twelfth and thirteenth centuries, not a biblical or rabbinic Name"},{native:"אצילות",translit:"atsilut",gloss:"emanation; the highest of the four worlds in the later scheme, and the term that distinguishes this model from creation out of nothing"},{native:"כתר",translit:"Keter",gloss:"Crown, the first sefirah; counted within the ten by some schools and placed above them by others, in which case Da'at fills the gap"},{native:"שכינה",translit:"Shekhinah",gloss:"the indwelling Presence; a rabbinic term which kabbalah identifies with Malkhut, the tenth and feminine sefirah, exiled with Israel"},{native:"סטרא אחרא",translit:"sitra aḥra",gloss:"the Other Side; the Zohar's Aramaic for the structured realm of impurity, which parallels the sefirot rather than merely opposing them"}],texts:[{title:"Sefer ha-Bahir",note:"Surfaces in Provence around the 1180s, ascribed to Nehunya ben ha-Kanah; the first text to treat the ten as divine powers and the first Jewish book to teach transmigration. Scholem's German edition, 1923; Daniel Abrams's critical edition, 1994."},{title:"The Zohar, Pritzker edition (Stanford, 2004–2017)",note:"Twelve volumes, translated by Daniel Matt with Nathan Wolski and Joel Hecker, from an eclectic Aramaic text Matt reconstructed as he went; the first complete scholarly translation into English, superseding the abridged Soncino version of 1931–34, which was itself made from the Aramaic rather than from Knorr’s Latin."},{title:"Nahmanides, Commentary on the Torah",note:"Kabbalistic readings introduced by the formula 'by way of truth', embedded in a mainstream exegetical work — the mechanism by which the new doctrine acquired standing."},{title:"Jacob Emden, Mitpachat Sefarim (Altona, 1768)",note:"Anachronisms in the Zohar catalogued from inside the tradition by a formidable rabbinic authority, who nonetheless held that an ancient core lay beneath the accretions."},{title:"Yehuda Liebes, 'How the Zohar Was Written', in Studies in the Zohar (1993)",note:"The case for a circle rather than a single author, and the standard modern point of departure for the authorship question in English."}]},{slug:"divine-names",title:"The Names and Their Keeping",native:"שם המפורש",translit:"Shem ha-Mephorash",summary:"A Name of four letters that stopped being spoken, one of forty-two hidden in a hymn, and one of seventy-two extracted from three verses of Exodus.",paragraphs:["The Tetragrammaton stands in the Hebrew Bible some six and a half thousand times, formed from the root of the verb to be and glossed at Exodus 3:14 by Ehyeh Asher Ehyeh — a form the Vulgate's ego sum qui sum and the Authorised Version's I AM narrow toward the present, where the Hebrew imperfect leans as readily toward the future. Its oldest surviving witness is not a scroll but an object: two rolled silver amulets excavated by Gabriel Barkay in 1979 from a burial cave at Ketef Hinnom outside Jerusalem, dated to the late seventh or early sixth century BCE, carrying a version of the priestly blessing with the Name in it. That is the oldest fragment of biblical text known. The Name was pronounced in the Temple as written and outside it by a substitute — Sotah 38a is explicit on the distinction — and Yoma 39b reports that after the death of Simeon the Righteous the priests ceased to bless with it. From then on Adonai is read where YHWH is written, the Masoretes point the consonants with Adonai's vowels to signal the substitution, and Christian readers who took the hybrid at face value produced Jehovah; the form is commonly credited to Petrus Galatinus's De Arcanis Catholicae Veritatis of 1518, with earlier claims made for the manuscript tradition of Raymond Martini's Pugio Fidei that remain disputed.","The Talmud is unusually forthcoming about the fact of longer Names and unusually silent about their contents. Kiddushin 71a records that the Name of twelve letters was once transmitted openly, then reserved to the discreet among the priests, who would swallow it into the melody of their brothers' chanting; and that the Name of forty-two letters is transmitted only to one who is modest and humble, past the midpoint of his years, not given to anger, not given to drink, and not insistent on his rights. Every criterion there is a criterion of character. Nothing in the passage discloses a letter, and the tradition's own framing is that the danger of these Names is a danger to the person who holds them.","The forty-two-letter Name has a medieval identification with the first forty-two letters of the Torah, from the bet of bereshit to the bet of bohu, and a liturgical one that is far better known. The hymn Ana be-Khoaḥ runs to seven lines of six words each, and the initials of its forty-two words yield the Name in seven groups. Its ascription to Nehunya ben ha-Kanah is traditional; the philological reckoning places the hymn in the Middle Ages. It is not an antiquarian curiosity: it stands in the standard prayer books, and is said in Kabbalat Shabbat, in the counting of the Omer, and in the daily service in various rites, by people who are not kabbalists and mostly do not read it as a Name at all.","The seventy-two is a piece of textual engineering. Exodus 14:19, 20 and 21 — the angel moving, the pillar of cloud, the sea driven back — each contain exactly seventy-two letters in the Masoretic text. Written one above the other with the middle verse reversed, and read down the columns, they yield seventy-two units of three letters. Rashi, commenting on Sukkah 45a, already knows a Name of seventy-two letters and derives the liturgical formula Ani va-Ho from it. A word of precision is owed here, because the terminology is routinely muddled: in rabbinic usage shem ha-mephorash means the Tetragrammaton pronounced explicitly, as at Sotah 38a, and its transfer to the seventy-two-letter construction is later. The two senses are now used almost interchangeably, and they are not the same thing.","What became of the seventy-two afterwards belongs to a different history and should be labelled as such. In the Christian Cabala and thence in Western occultism, the seventy-two three-letter units were given -el and -yah endings, turned into a hierarchy of seventy-two angels and distributed five degrees of the zodiac apiece — a scheme set out in Athanasius Kircher's Oedipus Aegyptiacus (Rome, 1652–54) and given its familiar popular form in Lenain's La Science cabalistique (Amiens, 1823), from which nineteenth-century occultism took it. In Jewish practice the Names went the other way, into liturgy and into objects: Shaddai on the mezuzah case and on the tefillin knot, whose value of 314 equals that of Metatron; the Tetragrammaton unpronounced at the centre of the service; and the amulets, which have their own current. The tradition's own instinct about all of this is conservative. It restricted the Names by character rather than by cleverness, and it kept the greatest of them by not saying it."],terms:[{native:"יהוה",translit:"YHWH",gloss:"the Tetragrammaton; yod-he-vav-he, summing to 26, unpronounced since antiquity and read as Adonai or, outside prayer, as ha-Shem, 'the Name'"},{native:"אדני",translit:"Adonai",gloss:"'my Lord'; the spoken substitute, whose vowels the Masoretes set on the Tetragrammaton's consonants — the hybrid Christian readers turned into Jehovah"},{native:"שם המפורש",translit:"shem ha-mephorash",gloss:"the explicit Name; in rabbinic usage the Tetragrammaton as pronounced in the Temple, and only later transferred to the seventy-two-letter construction"},{native:"אנא בכח",translit:"Ana be-Khoaḥ",gloss:"'We beg you, with the strength'; the seven-line hymn whose forty-two initials carry the forty-two-letter Name, ascribed by tradition to Nehunya ben ha-Kanah and dated by scholarship to the Middle Ages"},{native:"ויסע ויבא ויט",translit:"va-yissa va-yavo va-yeṭ",gloss:"the opening words of Exodus 14:19, 20 and 21, and the traditional shorthand for the seventy-two-letter Name drawn from those three verses of seventy-two letters each"},{native:"שדי",translit:"Shaddai",gloss:"Almighty; written on the mezuzah case and read as an acronym for the guardian of Israel's doors, and equal in value (314) to Metatron"}],texts:[{title:"Exodus 3:13–15",note:"Ehyeh Asher Ehyeh and the giving of the Name at the bush; the imperfect verb leans toward the future in a way the Greek, Latin and English versions all narrow."},{title:"BT Kiddushin 71a",note:"The Names of twelve and forty-two letters, and the conditions of transmission — every one of them a condition of character, with no letter of either Name disclosed."},{title:"Exodus 14:19–21",note:"Three consecutive verses of exactly seventy-two letters each; the source of the seventy-two-letter Name by a boustrophedon reading described in medieval sources and known to Rashi on Sukkah 45a."},{title:"The Ketef Hinnom silver amulets",note:"Excavated by Gabriel Barkay in 1979 and re-read with improved imaging in 2004; late seventh or early sixth century BCE, carrying the priestly blessing and the Tetragrammaton — the oldest surviving biblical text."},{title:"Eleazar of Worms, Sefer ha-Shem",note:"An early thirteenth-century treatise on the divine Names from the Hasidei Ashkenaz, and the fullest medieval Ashkenazi account of how the Names were understood to be constructed and guarded."}]},{slug:"abulafia",title:"The Science of Combination",native:"חכמת הצירוף",translit:"Ḥokhmat ha-Tseruf",summary:"A kabbalah of technique rather than doctrine — letters turned through their vowels until, its practitioners reported, something answered.",paragraphs:["Abraham Abulafia was born in Zaragoza in 1240 and spent his life moving. He went east in his twenties looking for the river Sambatyon and got as far as Acre before a war turned him back; he taught in Greece, in Capua, in Barcelona and in Sicily. In 1280 he went to Rome intending to summon Pope Nicholas III to the redemption of Israel; the pope died at Soriano the night before he arrived, and Abulafia was held for a fortnight and released. Solomon ibn Adret of Barcelona, the leading halakhic authority of the day, denounced him and issued a ban that kept his writings at the margins of the tradition for centuries. He wrote Sefer ha-Ot on the island of Comino between 1285 and 1288 and Imrei Shefer in 1291, after which he disappears from the record. His own dated colophons make him one of the few figures in this hall whose chronology is not guesswork.","What he taught was not a theosophy. Where the Castilian kabbalists mapped the divine life and asked how human action affects it, Abulafia asked what a person must do to receive prophecy, and answered with a method. Moshe Idel's distinction between an ecstatic or prophetic kabbalah and a theosophical-theurgical one was drawn largely to accommodate him, and it has held. His authority for the goal was Maimonides, whom he read as an esoteric writer and on whose Guide of the Perplexed he wrote three commentaries: prophecy as conjunction with the Active Intellect, arrived at by preparing the mind rather than by ritual. He is thus, oddly, the most rationalist-descended figure in the tradition and the most obviously ecstatic one.","The technique, as his books describe it, works on the letters of the divine Names. The practitioner writes out permutations — tseruf, the combining, from which the discipline takes its name — and runs each combination through the vowel series, then vocalises it, chanting with regulated breath and moving the head in the shape the vowel makes. Dillug and qefitzah, skipping and leaping, licence the mind to move between combinations without following a sequence. The purpose Abulafia gives is hattarat ha-qesharim, the untying of the knots that bind the soul to ordinary sense, so that the influx may be received. He is emphatic about the danger, warns that the unprepared are broken by it, and insists on preparation, seclusion and a teacher. All of this is set down here as his books set it down, which is the only way it belongs in a magazine.","There is one first-person account, and it is unusual in the whole of this literature. Sha'arei Tzedek, written about 1295 by a disciple whom Idel identified as Nathan ben Sa'adyah Har'ar, describes a training from the inside: the years of preliminary study, the failures, the night the letters began to move of their own accord, the sense of another presence speaking, and the writer's teacher telling him what to make of it and what not to. It is not a manual and not a doctrine; it is a report. Its value is that it lets a reader see what someone who did this thought was happening to him, which no amount of technical description supplies.","The ban did its work. Abulafia's books stayed in manuscript for six hundred years, and most were printed only from the 1990s, in Jerusalem editions that are useful rather than critical. The technique nevertheless travelled: Hayyim Vital reproduced Abulafian material in the fourth part of Sha'arei Qedushah, which was omitted from the printed editions and circulated in manuscript alone until the twentieth century — a striking measure of how uneasy the tradition remained about this material even when it kept it. Scholem gave Abulafia a lecture of his own in Major Trends in Jewish Mysticism in 1941; Idel's two monographs of 1988 and 1989 restored him as the second pole of medieval kabbalah, standing opposite the Zohar rather than beneath it. Almost everything in later Western esotericism that treats letters as a meditative instrument descends, by routes that are often untraceable, from this Sicilian corpus."],terms:[{native:"חכמת הצירוף",translit:"ḥokhmat ha-tseruf",gloss:"the science of combination; Abulafia's own name for his discipline, and the axis on which his kabbalah turns"},{native:"צירוף",translit:"tseruf",gloss:"combination, permutation; the operation of running the letters of a Name through their possible orders and vocalisations"},{native:"דילוג",translit:"dillug",gloss:"skipping; with qefitzah, leaping, the licence to move between combinations out of sequence rather than working through them in order"},{native:"התרת הקשרים",translit:"hattarat ha-qesharim",gloss:"the untying of the knots; Abulafia's phrase for loosing the soul from the bonds of ordinary sense, which he describes as the point of the exercise"},{native:"השכל הפועל",translit:"ha-sekhel ha-po'el",gloss:"the Active Intellect; the Maimonidean and Aristotelian term Abulafia keeps for what the prepared mind conjoins with"},{native:"נבואה",translit:"nevu'ah",gloss:"prophecy; the stated goal, which is why the school is called prophetic kabbalah — and the claim that drew Ibn Adret's ban"}],texts:[{title:"Abraham Abulafia, Or ha-Sekhel",note:"The fullest exposition of the method and of the divine Name it works upon; written in the 1280s and, like almost all his work, unprinted until modern times."},{title:"Abraham Abulafia, Ḥayyei ha-Olam ha-Ba",note:"His most copied book — surviving in more manuscripts than anything else he wrote — and the one closest to a practical exposition of the letter-combinations."},{title:"Abraham Abulafia, Sefer ha-Ot",note:"Written on Comino between 1285 and 1288; visionary, autobiographical and apocalyptic, and the book in which his own account of himself is most exposed."},{title:"Sha'arei Tzedek (c. 1295)",note:"An anonymous disciple's first-person narrative of the training and its effects, attributed by Moshe Idel to Nathan ben Sa'adyah Har'ar; the rarest kind of document in this field, a report rather than a doctrine."},{title:"Ḥayyim Vital, Sha'arei Qedushah, part four",note:"Abulafian technique carried into Safed a quarter of a millennium later, and then omitted from every printed edition, circulating in manuscript until the twentieth century."}]},{slug:"lurianic",title:"Contraction, Breaking, Repair",native:"צמצום",translit:"Tsimtsum",summary:"A cosmology in which God withdraws to make room, the vessels shatter under the light, and every ordinary religious act becomes salvage work.",paragraphs:["Isaac Luria was born in Jerusalem in 1534, grew up in Egypt, arrived in Safed in 1570 and died of plague in 1572, aged thirty-eight, having taught for roughly two years. He wrote almost nothing: a handful of Aramaic hymns for the three Sabbath meals, a commentary on the Sifra di-Tzeniuta, and little else. Everything that goes under his name is his disciples' record, and chiefly Hayyim Vital's, whose vast redaction — Etz Hayyim and the arrangement known as the Eight Gates — circulated in manuscript under restrictions and was not printed until the late eighteenth century. A rival transmission ran through Israel Sarug, whose claim to have studied with Luria Scholem rejected outright and Ronit Meroz has since argued deserves more credit; it matters because Sarug's version, not Vital's, is the one that reached Italy and from there Christian Europe. Anyone reading Lurianic kabbalah is reading a reconstruction, and which reconstruction is a live question.","Tzimtzum answers a question the earlier system had left standing: if Ein Sof is without limit and fills everything, where is there room for anything else? The answer is withdrawal. The Infinite contracts from a point within itself, leaving a vacated space — the ḥalal panuy, the tehiru — in which a world can be. A residue, the reshimu, remains in the emptied space like the scent in an emptied vessel, and into it a single ray, the qav, is drawn. The doctrinal cost of this is enormous and was understood to be so. Creation begins not with an act of giving but with an act of self-limitation, and on some readings exile is written into the structure of being before anything exists to be exiled.","What follows is catastrophe. Vessels are formed to hold the light that enters; the upper ones hold, but the seven below cannot bear what is poured into them and shatter. The light disperses, and the fragments — the sparks, counted as two hundred and eighty-eight by reading the merachefet of Genesis 1:2 as met rapaḥ — fall and are encased in the qelippot, the husks, which draw their existence from the holiness they imprison. Evil in this system is not a principle opposed to God but a consequence of a failure of containment, which is a considerably stranger idea. The repair is structural before it is moral: the sefirot are reconfigured as partzufim, countenances with internal relations — Arikh Anpin the Long Face, Abba and Imma, Ze'er Anpin and his Nuqva — so that what could not be held statically can be held dynamically, in relation.","Tikkun is the work of getting the sparks out. Every commandment performed with the proper kavvanah raises one; the soul's successive lives, its gilgulim, are assigned to the particular sparks it is answerable for; history is the duration of the salvage. This is the point at which a cosmological myth becomes a programme for the ordinary religious day, and it is the reason Lurianic kabbalah spread as no previous system had. It also has a dark reciprocal, since a doctrine in which sparks must be retrieved from the husks can be read as licensing descent into them. Nathan of Gaza read it exactly so after Sabbatai Zevi's apostasy in 1666, and the wreckage of that reading made kabbalah suspect to a great deal of rabbinic Europe for a century.","Three arguments should be given as arguments. First, Scholem held that the Lurianic myth is a response to the expulsion from Spain in 1492 — national exile projected onto the Godhead — and Moshe Idel has objected that the Safed kabbalists say remarkably little about the expulsion and that the myth's real diffusion is a seventeenth-century phenomenon. Second, whether tzimtzum is literal: the question divided the school of the Vilna Gaon from the Hasidim, and Shneur Zalman of Liady insists in the Tanya, at Sha'ar ha-Yiḥud ve-ha-Emunah chapter 7, that it is not to be understood as it sounds, since God's withdrawal from a place would leave that place without existence at all. Third, Scholem's further thesis that Lurianic kabbalah prepared the ground for Sabbatianism has been contested on the evidence of how little Lurianic material Sabbatai's early followers actually knew. And a note in the present tense: the Lurianic intentions are not a museum. They are the frame of the Nusaḥ ha-Ari used in Hasidic prayer, and the Beit El circle in Jerusalem, founded in 1737 and shaped by Shalom Sharabi, continues to pray with the kavvanot of this system."],terms:[{native:"צמצום",translit:"tsimtsum",gloss:"contraction; the withdrawal of the Infinite from a point to make room for a world — and the doctrine over which the Vilna Gaon's school and the Hasidim divided, on whether it is to be taken literally"},{native:"רשימו",translit:"reshimu",gloss:"the residue left in the vacated space after the withdrawal, as a scent remains in an emptied vessel"},{native:"קו",translit:"qav",gloss:"the line or ray of light drawn from Ein Sof into the vacated space; the single channel by which anything at all proceeds from the Infinite"},{native:"שבירת הכלים",translit:"shevirat ha-kelim",gloss:"the breaking of the vessels; the failure of the seven lower vessels to contain the light, and the origin of evil as a consequence rather than a principle"},{native:"קליפות",translit:"qelippot",gloss:"husks, shells; what encases the fallen sparks and draws its life from them — singular qelippah, and the same word as the peel of a fruit"},{native:"פרצוף",translit:"partsuf",gloss:"countenance, configuration; the reorganisation of the sefirot into interrelated personae — Arikh Anpin, Abba, Imma, Ze'er Anpin, Nuqva — after the breaking"}],texts:[{title:"Ḥayyim Vital, Etz Hayyim",note:"The principal redaction of Luria's teaching, arranged by Vital and his son Shmuel; restricted in circulation, copied under conditions, and not printed until the late eighteenth century."},{title:"Ḥayyim Vital, Sha'ar ha-Gilgulim",note:"The Gate of Transmigrations: souls, their roots, their previous lives and the sparks each is answerable for; where the system's cosmology becomes biography."},{title:"Naftali Bacharach, Emek ha-Melekh (Amsterdam, 1648)",note:"A systematic Lurianic exposition in the Sarugian line, widely read and widely criticised; the printed book through which much of Europe, Jewish and Christian alike, first met this material."},{title:"Shneur Zalman of Liady, Tanya, Sha'ar ha-Yiḥud ve-ha-Emunah, ch. 7",note:"The Hasidic insistence that tzimtzum is not to be read literally, set against the position associated with the school of the Vilna Gaon; the clearest statement of an argument still had."},{title:"Gershom Scholem, Major Trends in Jewish Mysticism (1941), seventh lecture; Moshe Idel, Kabbalah: New Perspectives (1988)",note:"Scholem's reading of the Lurianic myth as an answer to the expulsion of 1492, and Idel's objection that the Safed sources do not support it. Read together they show where the disagreement actually lies."}]},{slug:"christian-cabala",title:"The Cabala of the Christians",native:"קבלה נוצרית",translit:"Qabbalah Notsrit",summary:"Three centuries of Christian reading that changed the material it read — and left the diagram most Western esotericists still use.",paragraphs:["In December 1486 Giovanni Pico della Mirandola, aged twenty-three, published nine hundred theses for public disputation at Rome. Forty-seven were drawn from Jewish kabbalistic sources and seventy-one offered on his own authority, and among the magical conclusions stood the claim that has been quoted ever since: that no science better assures us of the divinity of Christ than magic and Kabbalah. The disputation never happened. A papal commission condemned thirteen theses, Pico's Apologia made matters worse, and Innocent VIII condemned all nine hundred in 1487. The claim outlived the condemnation. What Pico had done was to assert that a Jewish esoteric tradition, properly understood, testified to Christian truth — and that assertion founded a Christian Cabala that ran for three hundred years and is the ancestor of nearly everything an English-speaking reader is likely to have met under the name.","The foundation has a crack in it that should be stated first. Pico could not read the sources. He worked from Latin translations made for him at speed by Flavius Mithridates — Guglielmo Raimondo Moncada, a converted Sicilian Jew of formidable learning and unreliable habits. Chaim Wirszubski, in Pico della Mirandola's Encounter with Jewish Mysticism (1989), collated the translations against their Hebrew originals and showed that Mithridates interpolated Christological readings into them: material Pico took for the testimony of the Jewish tradition had been put there for him to find. Anyone tracing the chain back from a modern occult text through the Golden Dawn, through Knorr's Latin, to a Hebrew source, should know that the first link was already bent.","The doctrines the Christian cabalists wanted were specific. Chief among them was the pentagrammaton: insert a shin into the Tetragrammaton and the unpronounceable Name becomes YHShVH, which can be read as Jesus — Reuchlin's De Verbo Mirifico of 1494 makes this the hinge of the whole enterprise, the ineffable Name rendered speakable and effective by the Incarnation. Behind it came the reading of the upper sefirotic triad as trinitarian and of Adam Qadmon as Christ. It should be said plainly that much of this was conversionary in motive. It should also be said that Johannes Reuchlin, who gave the field its scholarship with De Arte Cabalistica in 1517, staked his career on defending Hebrew books against Johannes Pfefferkorn's campaign to burn them, and was prosecuted for it. Both things are true of the same movement.","The transmission chain is traceable book by book. Paolo Riccio's Portae Lucis (Augsburg, 1516), an abridged Latin version of Joseph Gikatilla's Sha'arei Orah, carries a woodcut of a bearded man holding a tree of ten circles: the first printed diagram of the sefirot, and the image that fixed the Tree in European minds. Francesco Giorgio's De Harmonia Mundi (1525) put it into a universal harmonic system; Cornelius Agrippa's De occulta philosophia (1533) put it into the third book of the standard magical handbook; Guillaume Postel translated the Zohar and the Bahir into Latin. Athanasius Kircher's Oedipus Aegyptiacus (Rome, 1652–54) supplied both the seventy-two-angel scheme and an arrangement of the twenty-two letters on the paths of the Tree — the Kircher Tree, still the standard arrangement in Western esotericism and not the arrangement of any Hebrew source. Then Christian Knorr von Rosenroth's Kabbala Denudata (Sulzbach, 1677; Frankfurt, 1684) put Zoharic and Lurianic material into Latin at length, and was read by Henry More, Francis Mercury van Helmont and Leibniz.","The nineteenth century made the material occult in the modern sense, and this is where the marking matters most. Éliphas Lévi, in Dogme et rituel de la haute magie (1854–56), paired the twenty-two letters with the twenty-two tarot trumps — an equation with no Jewish precedent whatever, invented in the nineteenth century and now so familiar that it is routinely mistaken for ancient. S. L. MacGregor Mathers's The Kabbalah Unveiled (1887) is an English translation of Knorr's Latin translation of the Aramaic Idrot, at two removes from the source. The Hermetic Order of the Golden Dawn, founded in 1888, built its system of correspondences on the Kircher Tree and Lévi's tarot equation, and the scheme was tabulated for the public in Crowley's Liber 777 of 1909 — which is the source of the Golden Dawn column in this hall's own alphabet, given there for readers of the Western tradition and labelled as such. That it is a living Western tradition making its own decisions, rather than a transmission of a Jewish one, is demonstrated by what happened next: Crowley revised part of the attribution on the authority of a line in Liber AL vel Legis, 'Tzaddi is not the Star', and much of the twentieth-century tradition followed him. The Hebrew manuscripts assign the seven doubles otherwise — the Gra–Ari text runs them in descending Chaldean order — and the recensions differ again from that."],terms:[{native:"קבלה",translit:"qabbalah",gloss:"reception, that which is received. The modern editorial convention of spelling it Kabbalah for the Jewish tradition, Cabala for the Christian and Qabalah for the Hermetic is a convenience of recent writers, not a historical distinction"},{native:"יהשוה",translit:"YHShVH",gloss:"the pentagrammaton: the Tetragrammaton with a shin inserted, read by Reuchlin as the Name made pronounceable in Jesus — the central Christian-Cabalistic construction, and one no Jewish source makes"},{native:"אדם קדמון",translit:"Adam Qadmon",gloss:"primordial Man; a Lurianic term for the first configuration after the withdrawal, read by Christian cabalists as Christ and transmitted in that sense into European philosophy through Knorr's Latin"},{native:"עץ החיים",translit:"ets ha-ḥayyim",gloss:"the Tree of Life; first printed as a diagram in Riccio's Portae Lucis of 1516, and arranged in the path-order Western esotericism uses by Kircher in the seventeenth century"},{native:"פרדס",translit:"pardes",gloss:"orchard; the four sages' garden at Hagigah 14b, taken medievally as an acronym for four levels of reading, and mapped by Christian readers onto their own fourfold sense of Scripture"},{native:"מלאך",translit:"mal'akh",gloss:"angel, messenger; the seventy-two angels built from the seventy-two-letter Name are a Christian-Cabalistic elaboration, systematised by Kircher and popularised in the nineteenth century"}],texts:[{title:"Giovanni Pico della Mirandola, Conclusiones nongentae (Rome, 1486)",note:"Nine hundred theses, of which forty-seven are drawn from kabbalistic sources and seventy-two are his own; condemned entire by Innocent VIII in 1487, and the founding act of the whole reception."},{title:"Chaim Wirszubski, Pico della Mirandola's Encounter with Jewish Mysticism (1989)",note:"The collation showing that Flavius Mithridates interpolated Christological readings into the translations Pico worked from. The single most important book for understanding what Christian Cabala actually received."},{title:"Paolo Riccio, Portae Lucis (Augsburg, 1516)",note:"An abridged Latin Gikatilla, whose frontispiece is the first printed diagram of the sefirotic tree — the image by which most Europeans have ever known the subject."},{title:"Christian Knorr von Rosenroth, Kabbala Denudata (Sulzbach, 1677; Frankfurt, 1684)",note:"Zoharic and Lurianic texts in Latin at length; the conduit through which the material reached Henry More, van Helmont, Leibniz and, two centuries later, the Golden Dawn."},{title:"S. L. MacGregor Mathers, The Kabbalah Unveiled (1887)",note:"The Idra Rabba, Idra Zuta and Sifra di-Tzeniuta in English — from Knorr's Latin, not from the Aramaic. The standard English source for late Victorian occultism, and at two removes from its text."}]},{slug:"practical-kabbalah",title:"The Practical Side",native:"קבלה מעשית",translit:"Qabbalah Ma'asit",summary:"Bowls buried upside down under thresholds, amulets that split a continent's rabbinate, and a Prague legend that turns out to be a hundred years old.",paragraphs:["The tradition itself distinguishes kabbalah iyyunit, the speculative kind, from kabbalah ma'asit, the practical — and in the surviving evidence the practical is the older of the two by centuries. Its earliest substantial monument is Sefer ha-Razim, the Book of the Mysteries, which sets out seven firmaments, the angelic hosts stationed in each, and the operations proper to them. The work was reconstructed by Mordecai Margalioth in 1966 from Cairo Genizah fragments and given a critical edition by Bill Rebiger and Peter Schäfer in 2009. It contains, in the fourth firmament, a prayer addressed to Helios written out in Hebrew letters — a Greek invocation transliterated rather than translated, on the working principle that a name must be pronounced as it is and not as it means. That is a fact about late-antique Jewish practice which no amount of later tidying removes.","The bowls are the largest body of physical evidence. From Sasanian Babylonia between roughly the fifth and eighth centuries survive earthenware bowls inscribed in a spiral running inward from the rim, in Jewish Babylonian Aramaic, Syriac and Mandaic, buried inverted under thresholds and in the corners of houses, most often to protect a named household against demons, curses and the illness of children. Some are drawn up in the legal form of a writ of divorce served upon Lilith, which is a striking transfer of a courtroom instrument into ritual use. James Montgomery's Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur (1913) remains the foundation of the field; Shaul Shaked, James Nathan Ford and Siam Bhayro's Aramaic Bowl Spells (2013) is the modern standard. A caution belongs with them: a large proportion of published bowls are unprovenanced, having reached scholarship through the antiquities market rather than through excavation, and whether such objects should be published at all is an unresolved dispute among the people who study them.","In the medieval Rhineland the Hasidei Ashkenaz — Judah he-Hasid, who died in 1217, and his student Eleazar of Worms, who died around 1230 — combined a demanding pietism with an intense preoccupation with the divine Names and with the letters of prayer. Sefer Hasidim, the movement's book of conduct, sits beside Eleazar's Sefer ha-Shem and his commentary on Sefer Yetzirah, and it is in the latter that the instructions for making a golem are set out: earth taken from virgin ground, and the recitation over it of the letters of the alphabet in combination with the letters of the Name. That corpus later fed into Sefer Raziel ha-Mal'akh, a composite printed at Amsterdam in 1701, whose subsequent career is instructive — the printed volume was kept in houses less to be read than to be present, the book itself functioning as the amulet.","The golem legend deserves its history stated straight, because it is the tradition's best example of how a story acquires an ancestor. The talmudic ground is Sanhedrin 65b, where Rava creates a man who cannot speak, and Rav Hanina and Rav Oshaia make a calf each Sabbath eve; the word golem itself comes from Psalm 139:16, an unformed thing. Eleazar of Worms gives procedures. Legends of an actual golem attach to Elijah Ba'al Shem of Chelm and are attested from the seventeenth century. But the famous story — Prague, clay from the Vltava, the shem placed in the mouth, the Sabbath forgotten, the rampage — is not attached to Judah Loew ben Bezalel, the Maharal, in any source before the nineteenth century, and its familiar narrative form was published in 1909 by Yudl Rosenberg as Nifla'ot Maharal, presented as an old manuscript and generally judged today to be Rosenberg's own composition. Gustav Meyrink's novel of 1915 and Paul Wegener's films completed the work. The Maharal himself was a considerable philosopher who never mentions the matter.","Amulets kept the practical tradition in ordinary life, and they were contested from within it throughout. Maimonides had dismissed the writers of amulets as fools in the Guide of the Perplexed I.61 and legislated against charms in the Mishneh Torah, and people wore them anyway. The ba'al shem — master of the Name — was a recognised occupation in early modern Ashkenaz, a healer and amulet-writer of whom Israel ben Eliezer, the Ba'al Shem Tov, is only the most consequential instance. What was at stake in a decipherment became brutally clear in 1751, when Jacob Emden read Sabbatian formulas out of the letters of amulets written by Jonathan Eybeschütz, rabbi of the Three Communities, and the accusation split European Jewry for years without ever being settled. And the practice has not stopped. Amulets, segullot, red threads and the blessings of practitioners remain part of Jewish life in many communities today, defended and criticised from inside the tradition on the same grounds they always were."],terms:[{native:"קבלה מעשית",translit:"qabbalah ma'asit",gloss:"practical kabbalah; the tradition's own name for the operative side, distinguished from kabbalah iyyunit, the speculative"},{native:"קמע",translit:"qame'a",gloss:"amulet; plural qeme'ot, and the ordinary object of the practical tradition — parchment or metal, carrying Names, verses and angelic names"},{native:"השבעה",translit:"hashba'ah",gloss:"adjuration; from the root to swear, the operative act of the Hekhalot and the bowls alike — a spirit bound by an oath sworn on names"},{native:"גולם",translit:"golem",gloss:"an unformed mass; the word appears once in the Bible, at Psalm 139:16, and only later comes to name the made man"},{native:"בעל שם",translit:"ba'al shem",gloss:"master of the Name; a recognised early modern occupation in Ashkenaz, healer and amulet-writer, of whom the Ba'al Shem Tov is the famous case"},{native:"לילית",translit:"Lilit",gloss:"Lilith; named once in Scripture at Isaiah 34:14, and the commonest addressee of the Babylonian bowls, some of which serve her with a writ of divorce"}],texts:[{title:"Sefer ha-Razim",note:"Seven firmaments with their angelic hosts and operations; reconstructed by Mordecai Margalioth in 1966 from Genizah fragments, critical edition by Bill Rebiger and Peter Schäfer (2009). Contains a prayer to Helios transliterated into Hebrew letters."},{title:"James Montgomery, Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur (1913); Shaul Shaked, James Nathan Ford and Siam Bhayro, Aramaic Bowl Spells (2013)",note:"The foundation and the current standard for the Babylonian bowls. The 2013 volume also sits inside the field's unresolved dispute over publishing unprovenanced material."},{title:"Eleazar of Worms, commentary on Sefer Yetzirah",note:"The Hasidei Ashkenaz's reading of the book as a manual of combination, and the source of the medieval golem procedures — recitation of the alphabet against the letters of the Name over earth."},{title:"Sefer Raziel ha-Mal'akh (Amsterdam, 1701)",note:"A composite of Ashkenazi and earlier material, printed and thereafter kept in houses as a protective object; the rare book whose physical presence was the point."},{title:"Yudl Rosenberg, Nifla'ot Maharal (1909)",note:"The Prague golem story in the form everyone knows it, presented as an old manuscript and generally judged Rosenberg's own composition. No source connects Judah Loew with a golem before the nineteenth century."}]}]};export{e as default};
